{
  "id": "pipeline-fault",
  "title": "A pipeline edit whose allocation fails partway on a real device is refused by name, disposes exactly what it created, leaves the installed graph presenting and running, and the same edit tried again installs",
  "binding": "Both legs boot the pipeline-feedback graph offscreen, pause, reset and step to submission 2 (2/16), when the instance owns exactly its installed graph, 49152 bytes, and wait for the SDF view's engine to be ready (world.wait ready), because the faults count every image any device service creates and an engine still building would take one. gpu.faults disarm restarts the creation counts and gpu.faults arm image 2 fails the second image any device service creates from then on, before it reaches the device. The corrected edit carries the installed half-float history and creates the six other images (gray and target per frame slot, 4096 bytes each), so its first image is created and its second fails: pipeline.wait installed fails with the GPU_CREATION_FAULT refusal naming image creation 2, gpu.faults list reads nothing armed after exactly two image creations, and pipeline.inspect reads the instance's 49152 bytes again, so the image the candidate made was released. The paused capture still reads 2/16 and one step renders 3/16 through the installed graph over its retained history. With the faults disarmed, pipeline.reload compiles the same edit again, which creates exactly its six images and installs while paused, and its step renders submission 4 as 1 - 4/16 = 0.75. Run with puck canary --debug-layers, the runner fails either leg on any validation message, and Direct3D 12's device teardown reports any object the refusal left alive as a [d3d12-debug] live line, which is one. The discriminating leg arms the seventh image, past the six the edit creates: nothing is refused, the edit installs beside the replaced graph's still-published images, so the instance owns more than 49152 bytes, and submission 3 renders 1 - 3/16 through the corrected graph, so the positive leg's refusal, ownership and step oracles turn red.",
  "bootShape": "offscreen",
  "backends": [
    "vulkan",
    "directx"
  ],
  "requirements": [
    "gpu"
  ],
  "timeoutSeconds": 60,
